WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.224
MCP is useful when an AI application needs current information or controlled access to external capabilities.

2
00:00:07.224 --> 00:00:26.897
Common examples include developer tools, code repositories, documentation lookup, file and knowledge-base access, ticketing systems, calendars and email at a high level, databases, cloud services, internal APIs, monitoring systems, business workflow automation, and local productivity tools.

3
00:00:26.897 --> 00:00:34.197
These use cases are broad, but they share a common need: the AI system must connect to something outside the model.

4
00:00:34.197 --> 00:00:36.733
Developer workflows are a natural fit.

5
00:00:36.733 --> 00:00:45.417
An assistant may read project documentation, inspect repository metadata, summarize issue history, or help draft a change request.

6
00:00:45.417 --> 00:00:47.953
Operations workflows are another fit.

7
00:00:47.953 --> 00:00:55.638
An assistant may retrieve monitoring context, summarize alerts, look up runbooks, or create a ticket for a human team.

8
00:00:55.638 --> 00:01:05.705
Business workflows may use approved access to calendars, documents, customer records, or internal knowledge bases to help users complete routine tasks.

9
00:01:05.705 --> 00:01:08.010
Some use cases should be read-only.

10
00:01:08.010 --> 00:01:16.694
Documentation lookup, status retrieval, schema inspection, and search can often provide value without changing external systems.

11
00:01:16.694 --> 00:01:20.998
Other use cases are write-capable and require stronger controls.

12
00:01:20.998 --> 00:01:31.526
Creating tickets, sending messages, modifying files, updating records, changing cloud resources, or triggering workflows can affect real people and systems.

13
00:01:31.526 --> 00:01:38.289
The difference between reading and acting should be obvious to the user and enforced by the application.

14
00:01:38.289 --> 00:01:41.516
The best use cases start with a clear job to be done.

15
00:01:41.516 --> 00:01:50.354
What does the user need, what data is required, what action is allowed, which identity should be used, and what evidence should be logged?

16
00:01:50.354 --> 00:01:58.500
Without that clarity, teams can end up connecting tools because they are available rather than because they are appropriate.

17
00:01:58.500 --> 00:02:09.566
MCP adoption works best when capabilities are tied to well-bounded workflows, not treated as a universal permission to let the model do anything connected systems can do.
